<article class="env-definition" id="09VU">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09VU">Definition <span data-tag="09VU">25.6.1</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a site. Assume $\mathcal{C}$ has equalizers and fibre products. Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a presheaf of sets. A <em>hypercovering of $\mathcal{G}$</em> is a simplicial object $K$ of $\text{SR}(\mathcal{C})$ endowed with an augmentation $F(K) \to \mathcal{G}$ such that </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$F(K_0) \to \mathcal{G}$ becomes surjective after sheafification, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$F(K_1) \to F(K_0) \times _\mathcal {G} F(K_0)$ becomes surjective after sheafification, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$F(K_{n + 1}) \longrightarrow F((\text{cosk}_ n \text{sk}_ n K)_{n + 1})$ for $n \geq 1$ becomes surjective after sheafification. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> We say that a simplicial object $K$ of $\text{SR}(\mathcal{C})$ is a <em>hypercovering</em> if $K$ is a hypercovering of the final object $*$ of $\textit{PSh}(\mathcal{C})$. </p>
</article>